## 4.5. Kinetic and adsorption isotherms
In a typical procedure $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of 1000 ppm NMP or DMF contaminated water was loaded on 10, 30, 50, 100, 200, 300 and $400~\mathrm{{mg}}$ adsorbent, the container was sealed, and placed in an incubator shaker at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $300\mathrm{rpm}$ for 24 hours. Samples from the supernatants were taken in order to quantify NMP and DMF. Samples were taken at 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 12 and 24 hours to obtain kinetic data.
## 4.6. Adsorbent recovery
Washing and drying methods were evaluated for adsorbent recovery. In a typical washing protocol $200~\mathrm{{mg}}$ adsorbent saturated with NMP was placed in an SPE cartridge and washed with $2.25~\mathrm{mL}$ washing solvent. 150 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ fractions were collected with a flow of $10~\mathrm{mL.min^{-1}}$ . The washing solvents were either methanol, water, 50 $10\%$ or $10\ \mathrm{vol\%}$ methanol in water. In a typical drying or vacuum regeneration procedure $200~\mathrm{mg}$ adsorbent saturated with NMP was placed under 0.01 mbar vacuum at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for approximately 12 hours. The weight of the adsorbent was measured at given intervals until no more change was observed. Note that using the Cox chart it can be estimated that the boiling point of NMP and DMF are $-5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ${}-20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively.46
## 4.7. Membrane fabrication
Polymer membranes were produced via phase inversion based on the protocol by Campbell et al.47 Dope solutions were formed by dissolving $25\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ of polyimide P84 in NMP followed by casting on to polypropylene non-woven sheets using a casting knife set to a thickness of $250~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ at a temperature of $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and a humidity of $35\mathrm{-}45\%$ . Membranes were cast using an automatic film applicator Elcometer 4340. The polymer membranes were then precipitated from solution via immersion into water bath (coagulation tank). The membranes were then placed in isopropyl alcohol (IPA) to remove water from the polymer matrix followed by crosslinking with $30~\mathrm{g.L^{-1}}$ solutions of hexane-1,6-diamine in IPA at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 hours. The crosslinked membranes were subsequently washed with IPA to remove excess crosslinking agent. To avoid pore collapse, the membranes were conditioned with a PEG400:IPA $\left(50:50\nu/\nu\right)$ solution for 4 hours then dried in air.
## 4.8. Membrane performance test
All filtration experiments were carried out at 10 bar using a cross-flow filtration system with an effective membrane area of $52\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The permeance of each membrane was calculated by dividing the acetone flux by the applied pressure. The model system for the solute rejection experiments comprised of a mixture of $1\ \mathrm{g.L}^{-1}\ \mathrm{PS}580$ and PS1300 polystyrene markers as well as $0.1\ \mathrm{g.L^{-1}}$ of methyl styrene dimer solution in acetone. The rejection (R) of markers was obtained by measuring the concentration of each polystyrene oligomer in the permeate and the feed, and calculating the ratio of the molecules retained by the membrane.
## 4.9. Continuous water treatment
Schematic representation of the continuous adsorption system is shown in Figure 6A. The system consists of two high pressure pumps (Gilson 305), a feed tank containing the wastewater from the coagulation bath; a wash tank containing methanol:water $(10{:}90~\nu/\nu)$ for column regeneration; and a vessel for the purified water and the waste solution; and two adsorption columns wet-packed with $50\ \mathrm{g}$ of MIP7. The high pressure pumps were used to feed the wastewater and the washing solution at a speed of $25~\mathrm{mL.min^{-1}}$ to the columns in an alternating manner. After the collection of $2.5\mathrm{~L~}$ purified water the columns were regenerated using $120~\mathrm{mL}$ washing solution. In total $12.5\mathrm{{L}}$ wastewater was purified continuously.
